messages.content:
{
  "results": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "snippet": "As a result, if a SOCKS5 proxy accepts the TCP connection but then stalls — a common occurrence with Tor circuit establishment — `recv()` blocks forever and the entire process hangs. The timeout passed to `httpx.Client(timeout=...)` has no effect because it is never forwarded to these `stream.read()` calls. The async backend does not have this bug — `_init_socks5_connection` in `socks_proxy.py` passes timeout through correctly. ## To reproduce ``` import httpx # Connect to a SOCKS5 proxy that accepts the TCP connection but stalls # on the handshake response (simulate with: nc -l 9150) proxy = ",
      "title": "_init_socks5_connection ignores timeout — SyncStream.read() puts socket into infinite blocking mode · Issue #1089 · encode/httpcore · GitHub",
      "url": "https://github.com/encode/httpcore/issues/1089"
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "snippet": "Any network glitch, load balancer timeout, or server-side hang causes the process to block forever. This is especially critical in autonomous/agentic loops where there is no human to notice and kill the process. ### Observed in production During an autonomous agent loop using `ChatOpenAI` against a local llama-server (OpenAI-compatible API), the process hung after 38 successful LLM calls. Server-side monitoring confirmed the server had finished generating (EOS after 180 tokens), but the client never closed the connection. The httpx client waited indefinitely because `timeout=None`. ### Root ca",
      "title": "ChatOpenAI/ChatAnthropic: request_timeout=None (default) disables SDK timeout, causing infinite hangs · Issue #35597 · langchain-ai/langchain · GitHub",
      "url": "https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain/issues/35597"
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "snippet": "opened on Jul 4, 2026 Issue body actions ## Summary The Telegram gateway adapter hangs indefinitely on \"Connecting to Telegram (attempt 1/8)...\" with `asyncio.wait_for(self._app.initialize(), timeout=30)` never raising a `TimeoutError`. The bot stays on attempt 1/8 forever, even after 15+ minutes. No error, no second attempt, no timeout. ## Environment Image: `nousresearch/hermes-agent:latest` Python: 3.13.5 httpx: 0.28.1 httpcore: 1.0.9 anyio: 4.12.1 s6-overlay v3 ## Symptom Log output: ``` [Telegram] Discovering Telegram API fallback IPs via DNS-over-HTTPS... [Telegram] Connecting to Telegra",
      "title": "Gateway hangs indefinitely on Telegram connect (attempt 1/8) — asyncio.wait_for never fires under s6 supervision · Issue #58236 · NousResearch/hermes-agent · GitHub",
      "url": "https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/issues/58236"
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "snippet": "loleg on May 3, 2025 More actions Update: with many thanks for support from @rattrayalex and team, and apologies for diverting from the performance discussion, I've mostly ruled out that the issue is with this package or with httpx. In my case an upgrade to `poetry 2.1.2` seems to have resolved a dependency conflict. Up and running with: ``` openai==1.76.2 h11==0.16.0 httpcore==1.0.9 httpx==0.28.1 ``` Reacted by nlsandler and Raman 24. ### markwitt1commentedon May 19, 2025 @markwitt1 markwitt1 on May 19, 2025 More actions Is this issue solved now in the latest version? 25. ### ishaan-jaffcomme",
      "title": "httpx client has very poor performance for concurrent requests compared to aiohttp · Issue #1596 · openai/openai-python · GitHub",
      "url": "https://github.com/openai/openai-python/issues/1596"
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "snippet": "| Describe the bug Cancelling an `engine.connect()` task twice while it recycles an existing asyncpg connection can permanently consume a pool slot. After the task finishes, the driver connection is closed, but the pool still reports one checked-out connection. With a one-slot pool, the next checkout times out. A single cancellation at the same point returns the slot correctly. This example uses standard asyncio, not AnyIO or an alternate event loop. Versions SQLAlchemy 2.0.54, and current `main` at `bc8982869d887a47a535ba45d80eb9a6f4595530` (reports 2.1.2). asyncpg 0.31.0; greenlet 3.5.6. CPy",
      "title": "Repeated cancellation during asyncpg connection recycling leaks a pool slot · sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy · Discussion #13620 · GitHub",
      "url": "https://github.com/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/discussions/13620"
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "snippet": "You must be logged in to vote All reactions 5 replies @andybbruno Comment options ### Uh oh! There was an error while loading. Please reload this page. ### Uh oh! There was an error while loading. Please reload this page. #### andybbruno Oct 22, 2024 Author | | | The problem is that I have a lot of httpx calls like these: ``` import httpx httpx get # no client here httpx post # no client here ``` So, the quickest way to modify all of them in one shot, was to set this timeout globally (using the `DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_CONFIG` constant). At the same time, however, I agree that modifying a constant is ",
      "title": "Proposal: Add a Global Timeout Setting in httpx · encode/httpx · Discussion #3356 · GitHub",
      "url": "https://github.com/encode/httpx/discussions/3356"
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "snippet": "@elprans elprans on Mar 18, 2020 Member More actions You seem to be missing the PL/pgSQL support in your PostgreSQL installation. The pool release logic requires PL/pgSQL to be functional. 5. ### dingguijincommentedon Mar 18, 2020 @dingguijin dingguijin on Mar 18, 2020 Author More actions Thanks, yes, release connection include a piece of PL/PGSQL script, but brew installation on Mac OS X not included the plpgsql lib. so this is not asyncpg issue. 6. dingguijin closed this as completedon Mar 18, 2020 Sign up for free to join this conversation on GitHub. Already have an account? Sign in to comm",
      "title": "connection pool demo not work! · Issue #542 · MagicStack/asyncpg · GitHub",
      "url": "https://github.com/MagicStack/asyncpg/issues/542"
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "snippet": "raise ex File \"d:\\ProgProject\\python\\learn_asyncpg\\venv\\lib\\site-packages\\asyncpg\\pool.py\", line 218, in release await self._con.reset(timeout=budget) File \"d:\\ProgProject\\python\\learn_asyncpg\\venv\\lib\\site-packages\\asyncpg\\connection.py\", line 1562, in reset await self.execute(reset_query) File \"d:\\ProgProject\\python\\learn_asyncpg\\venv\\lib\\site-packages\\asyncpg\\connection.py\", line 349, in execute result = await self._protocol.query(query, timeout) File \"asyncpg\\\\protocol\\\\protocol.pyx\", line 360, in query File \"asyncpg\\\\protocol\\\\protocol.pyx\", line 745, in asyncpg.protocol.protocol.BaseProt",
      "title": "How can I reuse the existing asyncpg connection pool?",
      "url": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79280433/how-can-i-reuse-the-existing-asyncpg-connection-pool"
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "snippet": "| I want to confirm whether the following is expected behavior, or whether SQLAlchemy would consider a defensive bound. I've already traced the root cause to the asyncpg driver and filed it there (MagicStack/asyncpg#1326) — this is not a claim that SQLAlchemy is doing something wrong. SQLAlchemy is correctly calling the driver's API; I'm asking about defense-in-depth. Versions: SQLAlchemy 2.0.44, asyncpg 0.31.0, PostgreSQL 16, CPython 3.11.11, Linux. Scenario A pooled async connection is established, then the network to the DB server is partitioned in a way that silently drops packets (no RST/",
      "title": "async pool_pre_ping / connection teardown is unbounded when the asyncpg DBAPI's cancel hangs — is a defensive timeout in scope? · sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy · Discussion #13338 · GitHub",
      "url": "https://github.com/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/discussions/13338"
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "snippet": "So this is offered as a corroborating data point rather than a ready-made repro: the value is that the crash site and the 0.30 → 0.31 boundary match this issue exactly, while the trigger here is coverage rather than Temporal. Both Temporal's sandbox and coverage install tracing/instrumentation, so \"a tracer is installed during connect\" looks like the common factor rather than anything Temporal-specific. Happy to run further diagnostics (coredump, `PYTHONFAULTHANDLER`, a debug build, bisect across 0.31.0 commits) if that would help narrow it. Reacted by Jakan 16. ### Jakan-Kinkcommentedon Aug 1",
      "title": "Segmentation fault on 0.31.0 when inside a Temporal activity · Issue #1292 · MagicStack/asyncpg · GitHub",
      "url": "https://github.com/MagicStack/asyncpg/issues/1292"
    }
  ]
}

